WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.780
Hi, I'm David Jackson,

2
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.720
an engineering manager on
the UI Frameworks team.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.300
Today, I'll cover how to
bring your app to iPhone Duo.

4
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.520
First, I'll tell you how
to get started in Xcode.

5
00:00:16.399 --> 00:00:19.260
Next, I'll walk through
general layout guidance to make

6
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.080
your app resize correctly
on any platform.

7
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:23.720
Then, I'll tell you about

8
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.736
specific layout
guidance for iPhone Duo

9
00:00:25.786 --> 00:00:27.160
to make your app truly shine.

10
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.340
Let's get started.

11
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.013
Your app will work

12
00:00:31.063 --> 00:00:33.740
on iPhone Duo even if
you haven't built it

13
00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:35.060
with the iOS 27 SDK.

14
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.443
When the device is closed,

15
00:00:37.493 --> 00:00:39.058
your app will use the screen space

16
00:00:39.108 --> 00:00:41.000
to the left of the
status bar and camera.

17
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:42.983
When the device is open,

18
00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:45.400
your app will be a familiar
size and aspect ratio.

19
00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.620
Here's how to take more advantage
of the available screen space.

20
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.505
If you've already
adopted the iOS 27 SDK,

21
00:00:51.555 --> 00:00:52.725
the work you've done

22
00:00:52.775 --> 00:00:55.560
to support iPhone app
resizing really pays off.

23
00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:57.200
Now your app will look like this.

24
00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:58.630
Your app will extend

25
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.160
to the left of the status bar
area on the inner display.

26
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.480
And here's how to take advantage
of the entire screen space.

27
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.320
When you build your app
with the iOS 27.1 SDK,

28
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:10.820
your app extends to
the edge of the screen.

29
00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.630
Standard navigation

30
00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:15.700
and toolbar buttons now lay out
vertically under the status bar.

31
00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:19.215
To get the full-screen experience,

32
00:01:19.265 --> 00:01:20.700
download Xcode 27.1.

33
00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.122
Choose the iPhone Duo simulator

34
00:01:23.172 --> 00:01:24.780
to run your app in Device Hub.

35
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.059
Use the control buttons at
the bottom of the screen

36
00:01:28.109 --> 00:01:29.032
to open,

37
00:01:29.082 --> 00:01:31.220
close, rotate, or fold iPhone Duo.

38
00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.737
Now that your app is using
the full screen space,

39
00:01:34.787 --> 00:01:37.020
you may notice some issues
with your layout in each pose.

40
00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.816
A great iPhone Duo
app follows flexible

41
00:01:39.866 --> 00:01:42.420
layout best practices we've
discussed in the past.

42
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.225
Most recently,

43
00:01:44.275 --> 00:01:47.100
we covered flexible layout
principles during the talk,

44
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.960
"Modernize Your UIKit
App" from WWDC26.

45
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.400
These principles matter more
than ever for iPhone Duo,

46
00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.260
as your app will need to
resize to fit every pose,

47
00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.500
opened, closed, rotated, or folded.

48
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.280
iPhone apps have been adapting
to change for a long time.

49
00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.927
They've adapted to new screen sizes,

50
00:02:05.977 --> 00:02:06.803
screen shapes,

51
00:02:06.853 --> 00:02:09.380
and special regions
like the Dynamic Island.

52
00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:12.052
iPhone Duo continues along that path

53
00:02:12.102 --> 00:02:14.300
with a new screen shape
and camera location.

54
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.765
With iOS 27,

55
00:02:16.815 --> 00:02:18.719
people can resize your app larger than

56
00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:21.080
ever before using iPhone
Mirroring on the Mac.

57
00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.280
Opening and closing iPhone
Duo works the same way.

58
00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.399
Your app may react to different
size class boundaries,

59
00:02:28.449 --> 00:02:29.760
but it is still an iPhone app.

60
00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:31.938
The best app layouts are

61
00:02:31.988 --> 00:02:34.000
adaptive across a
continuum of devices.

62
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.302
They avoid making assumptions
about display sizes

63
00:02:37.352 --> 00:02:39.980
or device capabilities based
on user interface idioms.

64
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.454
And they use tools like size classes

65
00:02:42.504 --> 00:02:44.600
to differentiate between
small and large layouts.

66
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:46.900
Let's talk more about size classes.

67
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.404
Horizontal and vertical size
classes are loosely tied

68
00:02:50.454 --> 00:02:52.620
to the available space in
their given dimensions.

69
00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:55.092
Size classes express the user

70
00:02:55.142 --> 00:02:56.850
experience your app
should provide based

71
00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:57.880
on the available space.

72
00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.560
For SwiftUI, use the environment.

73
00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:03.020
For UIKit, use trait collection.

74
00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.800
On iPhone Duo, there are
several size class combinations.

75
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.975
On the outer display,
like other iPhone models,

76
00:03:11.025 --> 00:03:13.839
iPhone Duo has regular
vertical size class and

77
00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.000
compact horizontal
size class in portrait,

78
00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.640
and compact vertical and horizontal
size classes in landscape.

79
00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:21.760
On the inner display,

80
00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.220
the additional space lets you
show more content like sidebars.

81
00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.420
As such, it has regular horizontal
and vertical size classes.

82
00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:31.624
The interface orientation

83
00:03:31.674 --> 00:03:34.320
on the outer display behaves
like any other iPhone.

84
00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.660
iPhone Duo is a great opportunity
to support landscape orientation,

85
00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.840
as people may want to set
the phone down like a tent.

86
00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.640
Interface orientation on the
inner display behaves differently.

87
00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:49.520
The inner display doesn't honor your
supported interface orientations.

88
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:51.500
As with Idiom,

89
00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:54.300
avoid checking interface
orientation for layout decisions.

90
00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.020
Use size classes instead.

91
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.500
On a device with two displays,

92
00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.000
avoid referencing the
main screen in your code.

93
00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.360
It's ambiguous and will be
deprecated in a future release.

94
00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:07.560
If possible, don't use
screen references at all.

95
00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.760
Instead, use more local
concepts like the environment,

96
00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.400
trait collection,
or the scene's bounds.

97
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:15.580
If you need access to the screen,

98
00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.640
access it dynamically
from the window scene.

99
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.459
To make sure your UI fits the
corners of the screen perfectly,

100
00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.040
use Concentricity APIs
introduced in iOS 26.

101
00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.660
They're updated to work with
the screen shapes on iPhone Duo.

102
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.200
For SwiftUI, use ConcentricRectangle.

103
00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.340
For UIKit, use UICornerConfiguration.

104
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.999
iPhone Duo will continue to honor

105
00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:40.480
the UIRequiresFullScreen key,

106
00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:42.571
but your app will still resize

107
00:04:42.621 --> 00:04:44.460
when someone opens or
closes their iPhone Duo.

108
00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:48.300
iPhone Duo respects your
supported interface orientations,

109
00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:51.080
but your app will scale
on the inner display,

110
00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:52.720
including in Split View multitasking.

111
00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.357
For more on iPhone Duo screens,

112
00:04:55.407 --> 00:04:56.920
see the companion Tech Talk,

113
00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.440
"Leverage multiple displays
and scenes on iPhone Duo."

114
00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.200
Now that your app is resizing to
fit the inner and outer displays,

115
00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.420
I'll talk about unique layout
considerations for iPhone Duo.

116
00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:10.980
Using standard navigation
patterns is a great way

117
00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:12.660
to adapt to every iPhone Duo pose.

118
00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:15.274
SwiftUI's NavigationSplitView

119
00:05:15.324 --> 00:05:18.380
or UIKit's UISplitViewController
are fully adaptive.

120
00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:20.832
When iPhone Duo is closed,

121
00:05:20.882 --> 00:05:23.340
columns will collapse to
single-stack navigation.

122
00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:25.050
When open,

123
00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.760
columns will appear both
tiled and as overlays.

124
00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:29.466
SwiftUI's TabView

125
00:05:29.516 --> 00:05:31.785
and UIKit's
UITabBarController also adapt

126
00:05:31.835 --> 00:05:32.900
to all poses.

127
00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.880
By default, tabs will appear
on the inner and outer display,

128
00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:38.920
laying out vertically
when appropriate.

129
00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.002
On the inner display,

130
00:05:41.052 --> 00:05:43.520
you can opt into a sidebar
with richer navigation.

131
00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.440
For SwiftUI, set the default
tab bar placement to sidebar.

132
00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.440
For UIKit, set the preferred
placement to sidebar.

133
00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:53.000
Sheets also adapt to all poses.

134
00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:56.380
On the outer display, sheets can
have buttons that lay out vertically.

135
00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:59.060
On the inner display,
sheets are centered.

136
00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.300
Other presentations like
popovers, context menus,

137
00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:04.700
and alerts also adapt to each pose.

138
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.022
Navigation bars, toolbars,

139
00:06:07.072 --> 00:06:09.440
and tab bars lay out
outside the safe area.

140
00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.480
They automatically avoid
system UI like the status bar

141
00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.340
and hardware features like the camera.

142
00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.120
Horizontal bars provide
top and bottom insets,

143
00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:21.220
while vertical bars provide
leading and trailing insets.

144
00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:24.120
For a deep dive on
vertically arranged content,

145
00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:27.580
check out the companion tech talk,
"Raise the Bar with iPhone Duo".

146
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.490
Respecting safe areas ensures

147
00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.240
your app's controls are reachable

148
00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:33.800
and your app is fully visible.

149
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.400
Here's how to best adopt
safe areas in your app.

150
00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:40.780
When positioning your UI,
make sure to place foreground elements

151
00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.360
like interactive controls
within the safe area.

152
00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:45.155
For SwiftUI,

153
00:06:45.205 --> 00:06:47.700
your content is placed within
the safe area by default.

154
00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.100
For UIKit, if you are
laying out views manually,

155
00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:53.720
reference the view's safe area insets,

156
00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.486
or use Auto Layout to
constrain the views

157
00:06:56.536 --> 00:06:58.260
to the safe area layout guide.

158
00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:01.560
Allow background elements
such as full-bleed artwork

159
00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:03.380
to fill the available space,

160
00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.940
extending behind
toolbars and sidebars.

161
00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.260
For SwiftUI, use ignoresSafeArea.

162
00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:11.880
For UIKit, use the view's bounds.

163
00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:15.220
Keep in mind that safe
areas are often asymmetric.

164
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.780
This is especially true on iPhone Duo.

165
00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:20.560
For example, vertical buttons can

166
00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.800
appear on the left side in landscape
and Split View multitasking.

167
00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:25.600
As such,

168
00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:28.460
avoid assuming that insets
on opposite sides are equal.

169
00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.060
Instead, write code that
handles each side independently.

170
00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:34.398
As with safe areas,

171
00:07:34.448 --> 00:07:36.640
layout margins are also asymmetric.

172
00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:39.383
This lets your foreground
content get closer

173
00:07:39.433 --> 00:07:41.100
to vertical buttons
and the status bar,

174
00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:43.260
while preserving the margin
on the opposite side.

175
00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:46.511
It's important to test
your handling of safe areas

176
00:07:46.561 --> 00:07:47.620
in different configurations.

177
00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:50.160
Split View multitasking
is no exception.

178
00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:53.400
Using Device Hub, preview
your app on the inner display.

179
00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:56.622
Drag your app using the
home indicator at the bottom

180
00:07:56.672 --> 00:07:57.840
to one side of the screen.

181
00:07:58.600 --> 00:07:59.960
An area to drop your app appears.

182
00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:03.120
Then, drag your app to the other side.

183
00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:06.700
Vertically laid-out content can
appear on either side of your app.

184
00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:09.277
Lastly, in iOS 27.1,

185
00:08:09.327 --> 00:08:10.942
we're introducing a new API

186
00:08:10.992 --> 00:08:12.668
that lets your custom UI use

187
00:08:12.718 --> 00:08:14.914
as much of the available screen space

188
00:08:14.964 --> 00:08:16.699
as possible without colliding

189
00:08:16.749 --> 00:08:18.180
with system-provided UI.

190
00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:21.123
For SwiftUI,

191
00:08:21.173 --> 00:08:22.100
use ReservedRegion

192
00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:24.879
to safely position UI
elements outside the safe area

193
00:08:24.929 --> 00:08:26.480
while maximizing usable space.

194
00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:30.320
For UIKit, it's called
UIViewReservedRegion.

195
00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:32.630
This is a great option

196
00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:34.305
for building your own custom bars

197
00:08:34.355 --> 00:08:36.180
or when implementing edge-to-edge UI.

198
00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:39.249
For more information
on reserved regions,

199
00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:40.721
including how you can use them

200
00:08:40.771 --> 00:08:42.820
to build custom UI that
adapts to the fold,

201
00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:44.885
see the companion Tech Talk,

202
00:08:44.935 --> 00:08:47.380
"Strike a Pose with Adaptive
Layouts on iPhone Duo."

203
00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:50.760
To summarize safe area best practices,

204
00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:52.100
use standard bars for UI

205
00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:54.480
that automatically
adapts to the safe area.

206
00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:56.163
Align your interactive

207
00:08:56.213 --> 00:08:58.640
or visible foreground
content to the safe area,

208
00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:01.740
while background content may
extend past the safe area.

209
00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:04.600
Make sure to account for, and test,

210
00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:07.100
asymmetrical safe areas
and layout margins.

211
00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:09.460
If you have more complex layouts,

212
00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:11.080
consider using reserved regions.

213
00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:14.580
I covered a lot of adaptive
layout best practices.

214
00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:17.840
Here's an efficient way to make
sure your app follows all of them.

215
00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:21.060
During the talk,
Modernize Your UIKit App,

216
00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.780
we introduced a new app
modernization skill.

217
00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:25.892
With Xcode 27.1,

218
00:09:25.942 --> 00:09:28.880
this skill has a new name:
App Resizability.

219
00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.320
It now supports
SwiftUI and iPhone Duo.

220
00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:33.970
Give it a try.

221
00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.540
Now your app is ready to feel
right at home on iPhone Duo.

222
00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.168
To recap, download Xcode 27.1,

223
00:09:41.218 --> 00:09:42.540
simulate your app

224
00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.020
on iPhone Duo using Device Hub,

225
00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.187
make sure your app follows all

226
00:09:47.237 --> 00:09:49.240
the adaptive layout best
practices I covered,

227
00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:51.260
and try out the App
Resizability skill.

228
00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:53.230
And that's it.

229
00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:54.420
Thank you so much for your time.

230
00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:57.673
I look forward to seeing your
iPhone Duo app above the fold

231
00:09:57.722 --> 00:09:58.620
on the App Store.
